From: MacFinals
Subject: [PATCH] server: STEAMDRIVER_BG_THREADS: below-normal threads in macOS's background band

Wine threads have no QoS class: the Mach policies apply_thread_priority sets opt a thread out of
QoS for good (pthread_set_qos_class_self_np then fails with EPERM, and the class reads 0), so
macOS places them by base priority alone. Below-normal threads get about 27, the utility band.
On chips with two kinds of performance cores that still spilled onto the fastest cluster: in THE
FINALS Unreal's below-normal background workers spent 35 % of their time (0.9 cores) there while
that cluster was full and clocked down (6 of 6 cores 84 % busy, 3.7 of 4.6 GHz) and the twelve
other cores were two-thirds idle (tests/rosetta_nt/R26_CPU_PROFILE.md).

With STEAMDRIVER_BG_THREADS=1 (read once from the wineserver's environment), threads whose base
priority is below normal get importance -12 or lower, a base priority under 20: the background
band. Off by default until an A/B in game says it helps.
---
--- a/server/thread.c
+++ b/server/thread.c
@@ -293,6 +293,27 @@
     return min + (effective_priority - 1) * range / 14;
 }
 
+/* STEAMDRIVER_BG_THREADS=1: threads below normal priority get a Mach base priority under 20,
+ * macOS's background band, instead of about 27 (its utility band).
+ *
+ * Wine threads have no QoS class (the policies set below opt a thread out of QoS for good), so
+ * macOS places them by base priority alone. On chips with two kinds of performance cores the
+ * utility band still spilled onto the fastest cluster: in THE FINALS Unreal's below-normal
+ * background workers spent 35 % of their time (0.9 cores) there while that cluster was full and
+ * clocked down (3.7 of 4.6 GHz) and the twelve other cores were two-thirds idle
+ * (tests/rosetta_nt/R26_CPU_PROFILE.md). The background band keeps them off it, leaving those
+ * cores and their clock to GameThread. Read once from the wineserver's environment. */
+static int bg_threads_enabled(void)
+{
+    static int enabled = -1;
+    if (enabled == -1)
+    {
+        const char *env = getenv( "STEAMDRIVER_BG_THREADS" );
+        enabled = env && env[0] == '1';
+    }
+    return enabled;
+}
+
 static void apply_thread_priority( struct thread *thread )
 {
     kern_return_t kr;
@@ -310,6 +331,10 @@
     /* effective priority 15 is for time-critical threads, so not compute-bound */
     thread_extended_policy.timeshare = effective_priority > 14 ? 0 : 1;
     thread_precedence_policy.importance = get_mach_importance( effective_priority );
+    /* base priority 31 + importance; under 20 is the background band */
+    if (bg_threads_enabled() && thread->base_priority < THREAD_PRIORITY_NORMAL &&
+        effective_priority < LOW_REALTIME_PRIORITY && thread_precedence_policy.importance > -12)
+        thread_precedence_policy.importance = -12;
     /* adapted from the QoS table at xnu/osfmk/kern/thread_policy.c */
     switch (thread->base_priority)
     {
